WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.070
Good morning, Hank. It's Sunday.

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:05.370
Yesterday, I was at the Indiana State Fair,
and I got to see the world's largest boar.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.910
He weighs more than eleven hundred pounds,
and he can't stand up because

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.860
his body is too big for his tiny, little legs.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.039
And what, you ask, is the name of the world's
largest boar?

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:15.540
Walkin' Tall.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.260
Get it? It's funny. Because he can't walk.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.470
Hank, Walkin' Tall - the world's largest boar,
who can't walk - reminded me a

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.650
little bit of America's healthcare system.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:28.240
Depending on your perspective, he's either
impressively huge, or ...

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.089
distressingly huge.

00:00:30.189 --> 00:00:31.939
[Music]

00:00:35.039 --> 00:00:37.790
So, what do you do when you have a pig that's
so big he can't walk?

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.759
You either kill him, put him on a diet, or
keep feedin' him,

00:00:41.859 --> 00:00:44.260
which is more or less what the healthcare
debate boils down to.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.099
So, Hank, I want to talk in a nonpartisan
way about the healthcare debate, but

00:00:47.199 --> 00:00:48.550
I don't want to scream,

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:50.949
and I don't want to act like the people watching
us have the intellectual

00:00:51.049 --> 00:00:53.139
sophistication of braindamaged goldfish,

00:00:53.439 --> 00:00:57.489
and, mostly, I don't wanna talk about death
panels because they're not in the bill,

00:00:57.589 --> 00:01:02.109
and the stupid, unsophisticated debate
about them has distracted

00:01:02.209 --> 00:01:05.000
from a really large and significant issue.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:08.150
Okay, Hank, so here are two things that pretty
much everyone conservative or liberal

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.480
agrees upon:

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:13.140
one, the United States spends way too much
of its gross domestic product

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:14.120
on healthcare;

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.169
and two, our current healthcare system is
radically unfair.

00:01:17.269 --> 00:01:20.150
I'm not saying that other models wouldn't
be more unfair, just that our current one

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:21.120
is unfair.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.150
Whether you have good healthcare in America
is dependent largely on

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:24.360
whether you work for a big company

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.720
(because big companies have to provide health benefits),

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.950
and second, whether you have a pre-existing
condition,

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:30.460
which a lot of times isn't your fault.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.900
Some people argue that we need a sort of large-scale, government-run

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:33.810
insurance company,

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:35.510
the so-called "Public Option",

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.300
which everyone would be able to buy into,
and poorer people would pay less

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:38.970
than richer people,

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:42.730
and then this huge, government-run insurer
would negotiate lower costs for procedures

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.960
and also cut down on the number
of procedures used.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.070
Now, even though the Public Option would not
in any way eliminate private insurers,

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.529
private insurers still hate the Public Option
because they know that the

00:01:52.629 --> 00:01:56.380
government-run insurance company will be larger
and, therefore, more able

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.379
to negotiate lower prices,

00:01:58.479 --> 00:02:00.929
which will, in turn, make the public insurance
less expensive,

00:02:01.029 --> 00:02:03.390
which will make people less inclined to use private insurance.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.810
Then, you have people who want to put the
pigs on a diet, who say that

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:09.790
instead of a public option, we can create
these sort of non-profit insurance

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:12.580
companies that will then have to compete with
private insurance,

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.830
and, because they won't be for-profit companies, they'll be incentivized

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:17.020
not to work for the shareholders in the company,

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:18.989
but instead to work for the patients they represent.

00:02:19.089 --> 00:02:21.750
Now, of course, the downside of the put-the-pig-on-the-diet plan is that you still end up with

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.450
tens of millions of people
who have no health insurance whatsoever.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:28.270
And then, there are the people who don't
want a healthcare system overhaul.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:29.460
They want to keep feeding the pig.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.590
A lot of those people are happy with the current
state of their healthcare and,

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:35.280
to be frank, I think they probably don't want
to pay for people who make

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:35.990
stupid decisions.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:37.620
And on some level, Hank, I can empathize
with this,

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.900
because when I was at the Indiana State Fair, and I saw chocolate-covered bacon,

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.160
I thought to myself:

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:45.800
"Geez! I don't really want to be on the financial
hook for people who eat

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:46.900
chocolate-covered bacon."

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.650
So, Hank, here's the underlying problem:

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:49.950
even though a lot of people are screaming,

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:52.300
"I don't want socialism! No socialism!"

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.719
in America right now, we have a lot of socialism.

00:02:54.919 --> 00:02:57.910
We share the cost of schools, and roads, and
the military.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.309
We also currently have socialized medicine;
we just have an outrageously

00:03:01.409 --> 00:03:04.230
bloated and inefficient system of socialized
medicine,

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:08.110
because there are hospitals in America where
anyone can go and get treatment.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:11.699
That treatment may eventually bankrupt you,
and it may be of poor quality,

00:03:11.799 --> 00:03:12.909
but you can get it,

00:03:13.009 --> 00:03:16.420
and I would argue that it is the inefficiency
of our socialized medicine that, in the end,

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:20.780
makes healthcare so much more
expensive than it is anywhere else in the world.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.280
Is healthcare a privilege, or is it a right?

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:27.480
If it's a privilege (even if it's a really desirable privilege, like indoor plumbing),

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.850
we need to stop giving healthcare of any kind to uninsured people

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:32.540
who can't pay for it in advance.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.290
But Hank, I have to tell you, I think the reason we continue to treat people who are uninsured

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.659
is because we don't believe that healthcare is a privilege.

00:03:39.759 --> 00:03:41.410
We believe that it is a right.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:44.280
And if it is a right - like life, liberty,
and the pursuit of happiness -

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.700
it is the responsibility of a government to protect
that right.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:48.610
I'll see you on Tuesday [DFTBA].

